speechtext:
The provincial officers would have the authority while they are provincial lists but they are, under this Bill, accepted as the basis of the federal lists and the final _ authority in urban centres would be the judges and in the rural centres the judges and the deputy returning officers when a man goes to the poll and asks for a vote. I do not quite agree with the statement of the leader of the Opposition. In the discussion which took place on Wednesday night there was no confusion in my mind about provincial or municipal lists. The first time I saw the amendment proposed by the hon. member for West Middlesex and saw the words "municipal lists" I said: "That won't go;
I will not agree to that?" When you propose to use provincial lists approved by a province and used' in the elections in a province that is one thing but when you propose to use municipal lists that is something entirely different. I think I said at the time that I would approve of provincial lists provided the age was limited and that is what I am prepared to abide by. There was a meeting held on Thursday morning and at that conference we reached an agreement. The leader of the Opposition, the hon. member for West Lamtoton (Mr. Pardee) and the hon. member for North Essex (Mr. Kennedy), all from Ontario, were at that conference. I raised the question that 1 have raised to-day. I think we left that conference virtually in agreement and I spent the necessary time to draw the amendments. They are pretty complicated amendments to draw, they entailed a great deal of difficulty but I thought that I had finally found a way out of i't. Now we are

met with the suggestion that whereas the province of Ontario has no provincial lists we should accept the municipal lists. The province of Saskatchewan has only municipal lists. There is no official provincial *"iist in the province of Saskatchewan until there is an election. That is the system which Premier Drury intends to adopt in the province of Ontario. I do not agree that we should wait on the whim or fancy of the Ontario Government in this respect. We are going a long distance when we say we will adopt the provincial lists as the basis of our federal franchise. We are going a long distance when we say that they may be two years old. We are going a very long distance when we drop out the original proposition of registration or enumeration in rural districts. Farther than that we have never been asked to go, and farther than that we do not purpose going. When you do give way the hon. member for Quebec Fast (Mr. Lapointe) gets up and says you have never accepted a suggestion or anything of the kind. He must have been in the House while this matter has been under consideration and he will remember that 1 .amended section 16, at the suggestion of the hon. member for George Etienne Cartier (Mr. Jacobs); I amended section 29 at the suggestion of the hon. member for North Waterloo (Mr. Euler); I amended section 28 in regard to the polling subdivisions at the request of the leader of the Opposition; I amended section 64 at the request of the leader of the Opposition; section 80 at the request of the leader of the Opposition and I propose to amend section 100, when we come to it, at the request of the hon. member for West Lambton (Mr. Pardee).
